/**
  @page guides-getting-started-core-concepts Understanding Core Concepts
  @brief Deep dive into meshes, finite elements, and variational formulations

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  This section provides a deeper understanding of the core concepts in Rodin,
  building on what you learned in the previous sections.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-geometry Geometry and Meshes

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-mesh The Mesh Object

  A @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh" is the fundamental geometric object in
  Rodin. It represents a discretization of your domain into simple shapes
  (polytopes).

  **Key concepts:**
  - **Vertices** (dimension 0): Points in space
  - **Edges/Faces** (dimension 1 in 2D, 1-2 in 3D): Line segments or faces
  - **Cells** (dimension 2 in 2D, 3 in 3D): Triangles, quads, tetrahedra, etc.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});

  std::cout << "Dimension: " << mesh.getDimension() << std::endl;
  std::cout << "Vertices: " << mesh.getVertexCount() << std::endl;
  std::cout << "Cells: " << mesh.getCellCount() << std::endl;
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-polytopes Polytopes

  A @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Polytope" is a geometric shape. Rodin
  supports various polytope types:

  | Type | Dimension | Description |
  |------|-----------|-------------|
  | `Point` | 0 | A point/vertex |
  | `Segment` | 1 | A line segment |
  | `Triangle` | 2 | A triangle |
  | `Quadrilateral` | 2 | A quadrilateral |
  | `Tetrahedron` | 3 | A tetrahedron |
  | `Hexahedron` | 3 | A hexahedron (cube) |
  | `Wedge` | 3 | A triangular prism |

  @code{.cpp}
  // Create different mesh types
  Mesh tri_mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {8, 8});
  Mesh tet_mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {4, 4, 4});
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-connectivity Connectivity

  Connectivity describes the relationships between polytopes of different
  dimensions. You need to compute connectivity before certain operations.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});

  // Face-to-cell connectivity (needed for boundary operations)
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);

  // Vertex-to-cell connectivity
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(0, 2);

  // Cell-to-cell connectivity (for finding neighbors)
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(2, 2);
  @endcode

  **Common connectivity patterns:**
  - `(1, 2)`: Edges to cells - needed for boundary conditions in 2D
  - `(2, 3)`: Faces to cells - needed for boundary conditions in 3D
  - `(0, d)`: Vertices to cells - for vertex-based operations
  - `(d, d)`: Cell-to-cell adjacency

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-iterators Mesh Iteration

  You can iterate over polytopes of a specific dimension:

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {8, 8});

  // Iterate over all cells
  for (auto it = mesh.getCell(); it; ++it) {
    // Access cell information
    std::cout << "Cell " << it->getIndex() << std::endl;
  }

  // Iterate over all vertices
  for (auto it = mesh.getVertex(); it; ++it) {
    Point p = it->getCoordinates();
    std::cout << "Vertex at (" << p.x() << ", " << p.y() << ")" << std::endl;
  }

  // Iterate over faces (edges in 2D)
  for (auto it = mesh.getFace(); it; ++it) {
    // Access face information
  }
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-variational Variational Formulations

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-fe-spaces Finite Element Spaces

  A **finite element space** @f$ V_h @f$ is a finite-dimensional subspace of a
  function space (such as @f$ H^1(\Omega) @f$ or @f$ L^2(\Omega) @f$), built by
  assembling local polynomial spaces on each cell of the mesh. In Rodin, finite
  element spaces are the bridge between the mesh geometry and the variational
  formulation of a PDE.

  Rodin provides the following standard spaces:

  | Space | Polynomial degree | Continuity | DOF location | Typical use |
  |-------|-------------------|------------|--------------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0" | 0 (constant) | Discontinuous | One per cell | Material indicators, piecewise constant data |
  | @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1" | 1 (linear) | @f$ C^0 @f$ continuous | One per vertex | Most scalar and vector PDEs |
  | @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1<k>" | @f$ k @f$ (arbitrary) | @f$ C^0 @f$ continuous | Vertices + edges + interior | Higher-order accuracy, mixed formulations |

  @note There is no separate `P2` class. Quadratic elements are obtained via
  `H1` with order 2: `H1 Vh(std::integral_constant<size_t, 2>{}, mesh)`.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});

  // Piecewise constant space: 1 DOF per cell
  P0 Wh(mesh);
  std::cout << "P0 DOFs: " << Wh.getSize() << std::endl;  // = number of cells

  // Piecewise linear space: 1 DOF per vertex
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  std::cout << "P1 DOFs: " << Vh.getSize() << std::endl;  // = number of vertices

  // Quadratic H1 space (order 2)
  H1 Qh(std::integral_constant<size_t, 2>{}, mesh);
  std::cout << "H1<2> DOFs: " << Qh.getSize() << std::endl;
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-trial-test Trial and Test Functions

  In the finite element method, we express problems in **weak form** using
  trial and test functions. These are abstract symbolic objects that represent
  functions in the finite element space — they do not store numerical values
  themselves.

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);

  // Trial function: represents the unknown solution u ∈ Vh
  TrialFunction u(Vh);

  // Test function: represents the weighting function v ∈ Vh
  TestFunction v(Vh);
  @endcode

  **Mathematical background:**

  The finite element method seeks an approximate solution @f$ u_h @f$ in the
  finite-dimensional space @f$ V_h @f$ by requiring the **residual** to be
  orthogonal to all test functions:

  @f[
    \text{Find } u_h \in V_h \text{ such that } a(u_h, v_h) = \ell(v_h)
    \quad \forall\, v_h \in V_h
  @f]

  - The **trial function** @f$ u_h @f$ (represented by `TrialFunction`) is the
    unknown being solved for. After solving, its coefficients are stored in the
    associated @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction", accessible
    via `u.getSolution()`.
  - The **test function** @f$ v_h @f$ (represented by `TestFunction`) is used
    to generate the equations of the discrete system. Each basis function
    @f$ \phi_i @f$ of @f$ V_h @f$ produces one equation @f$ a(u_h, \phi_i)
    = \ell(\phi_i) @f$, yielding the linear system @f$ A\mathbf{x}
    = \mathbf{b} @f$.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-operators Differential Operators

  Rodin provides differential operators that work on trial and test functions.
  These operators are **expression templates**: they are not evaluated
  immediately but are stored as symbolic expressions and evaluated during
  assembly.

  | Operator | Mathematical meaning | Result type |
  |----------|---------------------|-------------|
  | `Grad(u)` | @f$ \nabla u @f$ | Vector (for scalar @f$ u @f$) |
  | `Div(u)` | @f$ \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u} @f$ | Scalar (for vector @f$ \mathbf{u} @f$) |
  | `Jacobian(u)` | @f$ \nabla \mathbf{u} @f$ | Matrix (for vector @f$ \mathbf{u} @f$) |
  | `Transpose(A)` | @f$ A^T @f$ | Transpose of a matrix expression |
  | `Trace(A)` | @f$ \text{tr}(A) @f$ | Scalar (sum of diagonal entries) |
  | `Frobenius(A)` | @f$ \|A\|_F @f$ | Scalar (Frobenius norm) |
  | `Dot(a, b)` | @f$ \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} @f$ | Scalar (inner product or double contraction) |
  | `Component(u, i)` | @f$ u_i @f$ | Scalar (i-th component of vector @f$ \mathbf{u} @f$) |
  | `BoundaryNormal(mesh)` | @f$ \mathbf{n} @f$ | Outward unit normal on @f$ \partial\Omega @f$ |
  | `FaceNormal(mesh)` | @f$ \mathbf{n}_F @f$ | Unit normal on mesh faces (DG formulations) |
  | `IdentityMatrix(n)` | @f$ I_n @f$ | @f$ n \times n @f$ identity matrix |
  | `Potential(K, u)` | @f$ \int K(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) u(\mathbf{y}) \, d\mathbf{y} @f$ | Integral convolution (BEM/integral equations) |
  | `Flow(t, u, vel)` | @f$ u \circ \Phi_t @f$ | Flow composition (semi-Lagrangian advection) |
  | `TraceOperator(u, attr)` | @f$ u\|_{\partial\Omega} @f$ | Boundary trace (restriction to boundary) |

  @code{.cpp}
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction v(Vh);

  // The gradient of a scalar function is a vector
  auto grad_u = Grad(u);
  auto grad_v = Grad(v);

  // Used in integrals: the dot product is taken implicitly
  auto stiffness = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v));  // ∫ ∇u · ∇v dx
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-integrals Integrals

  The `Integral` function represents integration over the domain or boundary:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Domain integral: ∫_Ω ∇u · ∇v dx
  auto stiffness = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v));

  // Domain integral with function: ∫_Ω f v dx
  RealFunction f = 1.0;
  auto load = Integral(f, v);

  // The dot product is implicit for vector quantities
  auto inner_product = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v));
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-bc Boundary Conditions

  Rodin supports different types of boundary conditions:

  **Dirichlet (essential) boundary conditions:**
  @code{.cpp}
  // Homogeneous: u = 0 on boundary
  DirichletBC(u, Zero())

  // Non-homogeneous: u = g on boundary
  RealFunction g([](const Point& p) { return p.x(); });
  DirichletBC(u, g)

  // On specific boundary: u = 0 on boundary with attribute 1
  DirichletBC(u, Zero()).on(1)
  @endcode

  **Neumann (natural) boundary conditions:**
  @code{.cpp}
  // Neumann conditions appear as boundary integrals
  RealFunction g = 1.0;
  auto neumann = Integral(g, v).over(BoundaryAttribute(2));
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-problem Defining Problems

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-problem-assembly Problem Assembly

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::Problem "Problem" class represents a complete
  variational problem:

  @code{.cpp}
  Problem problem(u, v);
  problem = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))  // Bilinear form
          - Integral(f, v)               // Linear form
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());      // Boundary conditions
  @endcode

  **What happens internally:**
  1. Rodin assembles the stiffness matrix from `Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))`
  2. Assembles the load vector from `Integral(f, v)`
  3. Applies boundary conditions by modifying the system
  4. Prepares the linear system @f$ Ax = b @f$

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-solving Solving

  After defining a problem, solve it with an appropriate solver from the
  @ref Rodin::Solver "Solver" module. The most common pattern constructs the
  solver with the problem and calls `solve()` inline:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Inline: construct solver with problem, solve immediately
  Solver::CG(problem).solve();

  // Or: direct solver
  Solver::SparseLU(problem).solve();
  @endcode

  For finer control, construct the solver, configure it, then solve:

  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::CG solver(problem);
  solver.setMaxIterations(1000);
  solver.setTolerance(1e-10);
  solver.solve();

  if (solver.success())
    std::cout << "Converged." << std::endl;
  @endcode

  You can also pass a standalone solver to `problem.solve()`:

  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::SparseLU lu;
  problem.solve(lu);
  @endcode

  After solving, access the solution as a GridFunction:

  @code{.cpp}
  auto& solution = u.getSolution();
  @endcode

  The solution is stored in the @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction
  "GridFunction" associated with the trial function. You can evaluate it at
  any point, compute norms, or export it for visualization.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-workflow Typical Workflow

  Here's a summary of the typical workflow for solving a PDE with Rodin.
  Each step corresponds to one of the core abstractions:

  1. **Create or load a Mesh** — discretize the domain @f$ \Omega @f$
     @code{.cpp}
     Mesh mesh;
     mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
     mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);  // Needed for boundary detection
     @endcode

  2. **Choose a Finite Element Space** — define @f$ V_h @f$
     @code{.cpp}
     P1 Vh(mesh);                  // Piecewise linear
     // or: P0 Wh(mesh);           // Piecewise constant
     // or: H1 Vh(std::integral_constant<size_t, 2>{}, mesh);  // Quadratic
     @endcode

  3. **Create Trial and Test Functions** — the symbolic unknowns
     @code{.cpp}
     TrialFunction u(Vh);
     TestFunction v(Vh);
     @endcode

  4. **Define the Variational Problem** — translate the weak form
     @code{.cpp}
     Problem problem(u, v);
     problem = [bilinear forms] - [linear forms] + [boundary conditions];
     @endcode

  5. **Solve** — choose a solver and compute the solution
     @code{.cpp}
     Solver::CG(problem).solve();
     @endcode

  6. **Post-Process** — export for visualization
     @code{.cpp}
     IO::XDMF xdmf("Results");
     xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", u.getSolution());
     xdmf.write();
     @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gridfunctions Grid Functions

  A @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction" represents a discrete
  function in a finite element space — it stores the actual coefficient values
  (degrees of freedom) and provides evaluation, projection, and I/O.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-create Creating Grid Functions

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);

  // Create a grid function in the space (coefficients initialized to zero)
  GridFunction u(Vh);

  // Project a lambda expression onto the space
  u = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x() * p.y(); };

  // Project a RealFunction
  RealFunction f = 1;
  GridFunction v(Vh);
  v.project(f);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-access Accessing Values

  Individual degrees of freedom can be read or written using `operator[]`:

  @code{.cpp}
  GridFunction u(Vh);
  u[0] = 1.0;                  // Set DOF 0
  Real val = u[42];            // Read DOF 42
  @endcode

  The full coefficient vector is available via `getData()`:

  @code{.cpp}
  auto& coeffs = u.getData();  // Returns Math::Vector<Real>&
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-eval Evaluating at Points

  A grid function can be evaluated at any @ref Rodin::Geometry::Point "Point"
  on the mesh. The `getValue()` method performs finite element interpolation
  using the basis functions of the element containing the point:

  @code{.cpp}
  GridFunction u(Vh);
  u = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return std::sin(p.x()); };

  // Evaluate at a quadrature point during assembly (inside integrals)
  // or explicitly via getValue():
  // Real val = u.getValue(point);
  @endcode

  For **vector-valued** grid functions, component extraction is available:

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh, 2);              // 2D vector P1 space
  GridFunction u(Vh);
  auto ux = u.x();             // Component 0 as scalar function
  auto uy = u.y();             // Component 1 as scalar function
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-io Saving and Loading

  Grid functions can be saved and loaded in MFEM format:

  @code{.cpp}
  u.save("solution.gf", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  u.load("solution.gf", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-name Naming Grid Functions

  Assigning a name is required when adding grid functions to XDMF output:

  @code{.cpp}
  GridFunction u(Vh);
  u.setName("velocity");

  IO::XDMF xdmf("Output");
  auto grid = xdmf.grid();
  grid.setMesh(mesh);
  grid.add(u);                 // Uses the name "velocity"
  xdmf.write();
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions Function Classes

  Rodin provides several function classes for defining source terms,
  boundary data, and coefficients. All are expression templates that compose
  with integrals and boundary conditions.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions-real RealFunction

  @ref Rodin::Variational::RealFunction "RealFunction" represents a
  scalar-valued function @f$ f : \Omega \to \mathbb{R} @f$. It can be
  constructed from a constant, an integer, or a callable:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Constant function
  RealFunction f = 1;
  RealFunction g = 3.14;

  // Lambda function of the spatial coordinates
  RealFunction h([](const Geometry::Point& p) {
    return std::sin(M_PI * p.x()) * std::cos(M_PI * p.y());
  });
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions-vector VectorFunction

  @ref Rodin::Variational::VectorFunction "VectorFunction" represents a
  vector-valued function @f$ \mathbf{f} : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^d @f$.
  Components can be constants, RealFunctions, or mixed:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Constant vector
  VectorFunction gravity{0, -9.81};

  // Mixed: combine constants with symbolic expressions
  VectorFunction load{0, RealFunction([](const Geometry::Point& p) {
    return -std::sin(p.x());
  })};
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions-zero Zero

  @ref Rodin::Variational::Zero "Zero" is a class (not a free function) that
  produces the zero function. It supports both scalar and vector forms:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Scalar zero (CTAD: Zero() deduces Zero<Real>)
  DirichletBC(u, Zero())

  // Vector zero: Zero(dimension)
  DirichletBC(u, Zero(3))     // Zero vector in 3D
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions-math Mathematical Functions

  Rodin provides pointwise mathematical function operators that compose with
  any scalar function expression:

  | Function | Code | Mathematical meaning |
  |----------|------|---------------------|
  | Power | `Pow(f, n)` | @f$ f^n @f$ |
  | Exponential | `Exp(f)` | @f$ e^f @f$ |
  | Square root | `Sqrt(f)` | @f$ \sqrt{f} @f$ |
  | Sine | `Sin(f)` | @f$ \sin(f) @f$ |
  | Cosine | `Cos(f)` | @f$ \cos(f) @f$ |
  | Tangent | `Tan(f)` | @f$ \tan(f) @f$ |
  | Absolute value | `Abs(f)` | @f$ |f| @f$ |
  | Maximum | `Max(f, g)` | @f$ \max(f, g) @f$ |
  | Minimum | `Min(f, g)` | @f$ \min(f, g) @f$ |

  @code{.cpp}
  RealFunction u_exact([](const Geometry::Point& p) {
    return std::sin(M_PI * p.x());
  });
  // Or equivalently, composing Rodin operators:
  // auto u_exact = Sin(RealFunction([](const Geometry::Point& p) { ... }));
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions-coords Coordinate Functions

  The `F` namespace provides built-in coordinate projection functions that
  extract spatial coordinates at evaluation points. These are particularly
  convenient for defining spatially varying source terms and exact solutions:

  | Function | Description | Mathematical meaning |
  |----------|------------|---------------------|
  | `F::x` | @f$ x @f$-coordinate | @f$ x \mapsto x_1 @f$ |
  | `F::y` | @f$ y @f$-coordinate | @f$ x \mapsto x_2 @f$ |
  | `F::z` | @f$ z @f$-coordinate | @f$ x \mapsto x_3 @f$ |

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Variational/F.h>

  // Define a source term using coordinate functions
  auto f = cos(2 * pi() * F::x + pi() / 2) * sin(2 * pi() * F::y);

  // Boolean operations using coordinate functions
  GridFunction gf(Vh);
  gf = F::x > 0.5;  // 1 where x > 0.5, 0 elsewhere
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions-complex Complex Functions

  For complex-valued problems (e.g.\ Helmholtz, electromagnetics):

  | Function | Description |
  |----------|-------------|
  | `Re(u)` | Real part of complex-valued function |
  | `Im(u)` | Imaginary part of complex-valued function |
  | `Conjugate(u)` | Complex conjugate |

  @code{.cpp}
  P1<Complex> Vh(mesh);
  GridFunction gf(Vh);
  gf = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return Complex(p.x(), p.y()); };

  // Extract real and imaginary parts
  P1<Real> realVh(mesh);
  GridFunction re(realVh), im(realVh);
  re = Re(gf);
  im = Im(gf);
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-dg Discontinuous Galerkin Operators

  Rodin supports discontinuous Galerkin (DG) methods through several
  specialized operators and integral types. These are designed for problems
  where the finite element space is discontinuous across element boundaries
  (e.g.\ P0, or DG formulations with penalty terms).

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-dg-ops Jump and Average

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::Jump "Jump" and
  @ref Rodin::Variational::Average "Average" operators act on inter-element
  faces. For a function @f$ u @f$ with values @f$ u^+ @f$ and @f$ u^- @f$
  on the two sides of an internal face:

  | Operator | Definition | Code |
  |----------|-----------|------|
  | Jump | @f$ [u] = u^+ - u^- @f$ | `Jump(u)` |
  | Average | @f$ \{u\} = \frac{1}{2}(u^+ + u^-) @f$ | `Average(u)` |

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-dg-integrals Face and Interface Integrals

  In addition to `Integral` (cell interior) and `BoundaryIntegral`
  (boundary faces), Rodin provides:

  - @ref Rodin::Variational::FaceIntegral "FaceIntegral" — integrates over
    **all** faces (interior and boundary)
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::InterfaceIntegral "InterfaceIntegral" —
    integrates over **interior** faces only (excludes boundary)

  These are essential for DG penalty and consistency terms:

  @code{.cpp}
  // DG Poisson example (interior penalty method)
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  Real sigma = 10.0;  // penalty parameter

  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - InterfaceIntegral(Average(Grad(u)), Jump(v))
          - InterfaceIntegral(Jump(u), Average(Grad(v)))
          + InterfaceIntegral(sigma * Jump(u), Jump(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-advanced Advanced Operators

  Beyond the basic differential operators, Rodin provides several specialized
  operators for more advanced applications.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-flow Flow Operator

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::Flow "Flow" operator computes the composition
  @f$ u \circ \Phi @f$, where @f$ \Phi @f$ is the flow map generated by a
  velocity field @f$ \mathbf{v} @f$:
  @f[
    \frac{d\Phi}{dt}(\mathbf{x}, t) = \mathbf{v}(\Phi(\mathbf{x}, t), t), \quad
    \Phi(\mathbf{x}, 0) = \mathbf{x}
  @f]

  `Flow` traces characteristics backward in time and evaluates the function at
  the departure point. It is the core mechanism behind the semi-Lagrangian
  advection in the @ref Rodin::Advection "Advection module". The tracing uses
  robust cell-by-cell intersection with adaptive substepping and configurable
  boundary policies.

  @code{.cpp}
  VectorFunction velocity{1.0, 0.0};
  auto departure = Flow(-dt, phi, velocity);  // backward trace
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-potential Potential Operator

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::Potential "Potential" operator computes integral
  convolutions of the form:
  @f[
    u(\mathbf{x}) = \int_\Omega K(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) \, f(\mathbf{y}) \, d\mathbf{y}
  @f]
  where @f$ K @f$ is a kernel function. This is fundamental for boundary element
  methods (BEM) and integral equation formulations.

  @code{.cpp}
  // Newtonian potential (3D Laplace kernel)
  auto K = [](const Geometry::Point& x, const Geometry::Point& y) -> Real {
    return 1.0 / (4.0 * M_PI * (x.getPhysicalCoordinates() - y.getPhysicalCoordinates()).norm());
  };

  Problem eq(u, v);
  eq = Integral(0.0001 * Grad(u), Grad(v))
     + Integral(Potential(K, u), v)
     - Integral(v);
  @endcode

  @note The kernel @f$ K(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) @f$ is singular when
  @f$ \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{y} @f$. The small regularization term
  @f$ \varepsilon \int \nabla u \cdot \nabla v @f$ stabilizes the formulation.
  See the `examples/IntegralEquations/` directory for complete implementations.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-trace TraceOperator

  The `TraceOperator` restricts a function to a specified mesh boundary:
  @f$ u|_{\partial\Omega} @f$. For @f$ u \in H^1(\Omega) @f$, the trace belongs
  to @f$ H^{1/2}(\partial\Omega) @f$.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto u_boundary = TraceOperator(u, boundaryAttribute);
  @endcode

